The Lab
Homeschooling with Necessity — Next Steps & Research Blueprint
Purpose: Preserve the ideas and research gathered for the next development phase of The Lab so the project can be resumed later without losing the plan.
Core vision: The Lab should become more than a webpage. It should grow into a practical, searchable library of real-world investigations connecting science, math, engineering, literacy, homesteading, off-grid skills, life skills, and practical economics.
1. Turn The Lab into a Project Library
Create individual project pages rather than keeping everything on one landing page.
Organize projects by topic: Experiments & Research; Homestead & Off-Grid Skills; Practical Economics; Engineering & Design; Weather & Earth Systems; Gardening & Food Systems; Energy; Water; Household Systems.
Add filters for approximate age/grade band, subject connections, difficulty, indoor/outdoor, materials needed, and estimated time.
Use a consistent project-page template so new projects can be added quickly.
2. Build a Standard HWN Lab Project Template
Project title and a short real-world hook.
Big question or problem.
Learning objectives and subject connections.
Suggested age/grade band and prerequisite skills.
Materials and preparation.
Safety notes and adult-supervision requirements where applicable.
Prediction or prior-knowledge prompt.
Investigation/design procedure.
Data table or observation sheet.
Graphing/math connection.
Analysis questions.
Conclusion and reflection.
Extension/challenge option.
Portfolio evidence checklist.
Optional standards alignment.
3. Create a Downloadable Lab Notebook System
Make printable and digital-friendly pages for Question, Prediction, Plan, Evidence, Analysis, Conclusion,
and Next Step.
Create versions for younger learners and older students.
Add optional graph paper, data tables, observation logs, project reflection, and presentation pages.
Design the notebook so completed projects can become homeschool portfolio evidence.
4. Develop a Real-World Project Sequence
Begin with simple observation and measurement projects.
Progress into controlled investigations and data analysis.
Move into engineering/design challenges involving criteria, constraints, testing, and revision.
Introduce systems projects where learners study inputs, outputs, waste, bottlenecks, and trade-offs.
Add increasingly sophisticated economics projects: budgets, unit costs, pricing, market research, revenue/expense tracking, and break-even reasoning.
5. Make Homesteading an Academic Laboratory
Garden projects: germination, soil observations, plant needs, seasonal data, pollinators, productivity, and
resource use.
Water projects: water-cycle observations, conservation, flow, measurement, and filtration concepts using safe test water.
Energy projects: energy sources, electricity concepts, solar observations, efficiency, watts/watt-hours, and data collection.
Systems projects: map how resources enter a household or homestead, how they are transformed, and where waste or inefficiency occurs.
Animal-related projects should remain age-appropriate and include clear safety boundaries and responsible care.
6. Build the Practical Economics Track
Use realistic scenarios rather than abstract worksheets whenever possible.
Create budgeting and comparison-shopping investigations.
Develop a Cost of Production project for something made, grown, or planned.
Create a Small-Business Lab covering customer needs, pricing, expenses, revenue, and simple break-even reasoning.
Add simulations and case studies so students practice making decisions and explaining trade-offs.
Keep financial activities educational and age-appropriate; avoid presenting investing or financial products as required activities.
7. Cross-Connect Every Project to Multiple Subjects
Science: phenomena, evidence, systems, energy, Earth/life/physical science.
Math: measurement, ratios, rates, percentages, graphing, statistics, geometry, unit conversions, and budgets.
Language Arts: research, technical vocabulary, lab notes, explanations, claims/evidence, reports, and presentations.
Technology/Engineering: design, prototyping, troubleshooting, data tools, and systems thinking.
Life Skills: planning, resource management, decision-making, persistence, communication, and problem solving.
8. Add a Standards-Aligned Research Layer
Map projects to NGSS science and engineering practices and relevant disciplinary/crosscutting
concepts.
Use Missouri Learning Standards as a reference point for Missouri families, while keeping the Lab flexible rather than pretending every project is a full curriculum.
For financial education, use CFPB's research-based building blocks and age-appropriate teaching strategies.
Where useful, add standards codes only after checking the exact current standard wording.
9. Create a Searchable Lab Index
Search by subject, age/grade, skill, topic, project length, materials, indoor/outdoor, and homestead
connection.
Give every project a short URL-friendly identifier.
Use tags consistently so the library remains maintainable as it grows.
Eventually add a 'Surprise Me' or 'Find a Project' feature based on learner age, time available, and available materials.
10. Build a Portfolio/Record-Keeping Connection
Add a printable project completion sheet.
Provide a suggested list of evidence: notes, photos, measurements, graphs, drafts, final explanation, and reflection.
Allow one project to document learning in several subject areas without forcing artificial subject boundaries.
Consider a future project log that families can use to track completed Lab work over a school year.
11. Future Interactive Features
Interactive data tables and graphing.
Project checklists.
Printable/downloadable experiment sheets.
Teacher/parent preparation notes.
Student-facing and instructor-facing views.
Project difficulty indicators.
A growing library of original HWN experiments and investigations.
Potential future community submissions, with careful review before publication.
Research Foundation
The Lab's design is grounded in the following official sources and research directions reviewed during development.
Next Generation Science Standards (NGSS)
NGSS describes science and engineering practices as what students do to make sense of phenomena and design/build systems. Its three-dimensional approach combines science and engineering practices, crosscutting concepts, and disciplinary core ideas. The standards site also emphasizes learning science by doing science.
Source: https://www.nextgenscience.org/ | https://www.nextgenscience.org/get-know-standards | https://www.nextgenscience.org/glossary/science-and-engineering-practices-sep
Missouri Learning Standards — Missouri DESE
Missouri DESE explains that the standards emphasize both knowledge/skills and the ability to apply them to problems and decisions likely to be encountered after graduation. DESE also states that the standards do not dictate curriculum, leaving curriculum and instructional decisions to local districts and schools.
Source: https://dese.mo.gov/college-career-readiness/curriculum/missouri-learning-standards
Consumer Financial Protection Bureau — Youth Financial Education
CFPB provides K–12 financial education resources and a developmental framework for youth financial capability. Its materials identify executive function, financial habits and norms, and financial knowledge/decision-making as interconnected building blocks.
Source: https://www.consumerfinance.gov/consumer-tools/educator-tools/youth-financial-education/ | https://www.consumerfinance.gov/consumer-tools/educator-tools/youth-financial-education/learn/
CFPB — Financial Knowledge and Decision-Making
CFPB specifically identifies project-based learning, simulations, and real-world case studies as useful approaches for developing financial knowledge and decision-making skills.
Source: https://www.consumerfinance.gov/consumer-tools/educator-tools/youth-financial-education/learn/financial-knowledge-decisio n-making-skills/
U.S. Department of Energy — Solar Classroom Lesson Plan
DOE provides a practical example of using solar energy as a hands-on educational project, including learning solar basics and moving into experimentation and project work.
Source: https://www.energy.gov/articles/solar-classroom-lesson-plan
Recommended Development Order
1 1. Finish and visually review the current The Lab landing page.
2 2. Create the reusable project-page template.
3 3. Build the first 6–10 flagship projects across different Lab categories.
4 4. Create the printable HWN Lab Notebook pages.
5 5. Add standards/subject mappings to the flagship projects.
6 6. Build the searchable/index page once enough projects exist to justify filtering.
7 7. Expand the library gradually, prioritizing original, practical projects that fit the HWN mission.
Important note for future development
This document is a planning/research snapshot, not a finished curriculum. Before publishing standards codes, legal/academic compliance claims, safety procedures, or time-sensitive resource recommendations, verify the current official source again. The Lab should remain flexible, practical, inclusive, and usable alongside a family's chosen homeschool curriculum.
